**`PAYMENTS_SETTLEMENT_MODE`** is a configuration value that controls how `chargeOrder()` writes ledger rows. Under `legacy` it writes one row per order, and under `split` it writes two. Release `v412` changed it from `legacy` to `split`, and that was the trigger: the config bundle was published while all four instances still ran `v411`. `v411`'s `assertSettlementMode()` throws on any value it doesn't recognise, including `split`.

**Migration `0042_add_settlement_currency`** was the second step of the `v412` release and ran at 14:05. In one transaction it adds a `settlement_currency char(3)` column to `ledger_entries`, fills existing rows with `'USD'`, and then sets the column `NOT NULL` with no default:

```sql
ALTER TABLE ledger_entries ADD COLUMN settlement_currency char(3);
UPDATE ledger_entries SET settlement_currency = 'USD';
ALTER TABLE ledger_entries ALTER COLUMN settlement_currency SET NOT NULL;
```

`v411` code never writes that column, so once the migration had run, every `v411` insert failed with a not-null violation. That is why reverting the config at 14:21 and rolling back to `v411` at 14:33 both failed. The only fix was to roll forward to `v412`.

